---
/** Which readings each wearable writes to Apple Health and Health Connect, and so to Verve. Rendered by /science/sleep-heart/wearables; see scienceTopics.ts. */
import { topicHref } from "../../data/scienceTopics";
const connect = "/science/sleep-heart/sleep-rhythm#connect-wearables";
const whatWritesWhat = "/science/sleep-heart/sleep-rhythm#what-writes-what";
const energyWithRest = `${topicHref("weekly-volume")}#energy-with-rest`;
const nightHrv = topicHref("night-hrv");
const activityLevel = topicHref("activity-level");
const steps = topicHref("steps");

import { WEARABLE_ROWS as rows, WEARABLE_COLS as cols, WEARABLE_COL_IDS as colIds } from "../../data/wearables";
---

<p class="mt-6 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  Verve reads the phone&rsquo;s health store and nothing else: Apple Health on an iPhone, Health
  Connect on Android. A reading reaches it only when the wearable&rsquo;s own app has written it
  there. An Apple Watch writes to Apple Health alone and a Galaxy Watch to Health Connect alone;
  WHOOP, Oura, Garmin and the Google Health app behind Fitbit and Pixel Watch write to both, each
  its own list. The table is those lists, reading by reading, against what Verve does with each.
  Type a wearable&rsquo;s name into the search on the <a href="/science">science hub</a> to land on
  its column; <a href={connect}>how to connect</a> has the switches to turn on.
</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  <strong class="text-text-primary">How to read it.</strong> &ldquo;Not listed&rdquo; means the reading
  is not on the maker&rsquo;s own published list for that store when it was checked, on 2 and 3
  November 2026; an app may still write it, and Verve reads whatever is there. &ldquo;Not
  confirmed&rdquo; means the maker publishes no list for that store. Settings &rarr; Data sources in
  the app names every writer Verve has actually seen on your phone, which is the answer for your
  own devices.
</p>

<div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-ink/10 bg-well">
  <table class="w-full min-w-[1180px] text-[12px]">
    <thead>
      <tr class="text-left font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-text-tertiary">
        {cols.map((c, i) => (
          <th class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold">{colIds[i] ? <a href={`#${colIds[i]}`} class="hover:text-text-primary">{c}</a> : c}</th>
        ))}
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody class="divide-y divide-ink/[0.06]">
      {rows.map(([r, watch, whoop, oura, garmin, fitbit, galaxy, verve]) => (
        <tr class="align-top">
          <td class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold text-text-primary">{r}</td>
          <td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">{watch}</td>
          <td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">{whoop}</td>
          <td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">{oura}</td>
          <td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">{garmin}</td>
          <td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">{fitbit}</td>
          <td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">{galaxy}</td>
          <td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-tertiary">{verve}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<h3 id="apple-watch" class="scroll-mt-8 vrv-anchor mt-10 pt-2 text-lg font-semibold text-text-primary">Apple Watch</h3>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  The full list, to Apple Health only: workouts with heart rate and active energy, heart rate
  through the day and the night, resting heart rate, night HRV as SDNN, sleep with its stages,
  breathing rate, blood oxygen where the model measures it, sleeping wrist temperature from Series
  8, cardio fitness, steps and distance by the minute, stand minutes, and resting energy beside
  the active. Its active energy is the burn above rest, so Verve adds the resting MET before a
  session is classed (<a href={energyWithRest}>why</a>). Nothing from it reaches an Android phone.
  Source: Apple&rsquo;s own Health app documentation, read 1 November 2026.
</p>

<h3 id="whoop" class="scroll-mt-8 vrv-anchor mt-10 pt-2 text-lg font-semibold text-text-primary">WHOOP</h3>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  To Apple Health, by WHOOP&rsquo;s own article: workouts, heart rate inside them, sleep as asleep and
  awake intervals, resting heart rate, breathing rate, blood oxygen, and steps as one block for the
  day written the morning after. To Health Connect, the same list, with weight and lean mass added;
  Verve checked the permission list on a Motorola on 27 October 2026 and found it so. In its own
  app and nowhere else: HRV, recovery, strain, skin temperature, sleep stages, and VO&#8322;max. Its
  one calorie figure per activity already holds the resting burn, so Verve reads it as it is: the
  founder&rsquo;s walk of 2 November was 232 kcal in the WHOOP app and 232 active, 232 total, in
  Health. In Verve a WHOOP gives sleep with efficiency and wakes, resting heart rate, breathing,
  blood oxygen, started workouts with Zone 2 and the dose, and a steps total; it gives no HRV, no
  stages, no physical activity level, and no cadence or sitting hours unless the phone counts steps
  in a pocket. Sources:
  <a href="https://support.whoop.com/s/article/Apple-Health-Integration?language=en_US" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WHOOP, Apple Health integration</a>;
  <a href="https://support.whoop.com/s/article/Google-Health-Integration-For-Android?language=en_US" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WHOOP, Health Connect for Android</a>;
  <a href={whatWritesWhat}>the night&rsquo;s readings on both phones</a>.
</p>

<h3 id="oura" class="scroll-mt-8 vrv-anchor mt-10 pt-2 text-lg font-semibold text-text-primary">Oura</h3>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  To Apple Health, by Oura&rsquo;s article: sleep with start, end, duration and stages, heart rate at
  one-minute intervals, breathing rate, active energy, steps, weight and height, mindful minutes
  and workouts. HRV is not on that list, and VO&#8322;max flows the other way: Oura imports
  Apple&rsquo;s cardio fitness. To Health Connect: active calories, distance, exercise and steps;
  body measurements; sleep with stages; heart rate and HRV. So on Android a ring gives Verve a
  night HRV baseline (rMSSD, named as such, never mixed with a Watch&rsquo;s SDNN, see
  <a href={nightHrv}>Night HRV</a>) and on an iPhone it does not. Readiness, temperature deviation
  and blood oxygen stay in the Oura app. Sources:
  <a href="https://support.ouraring.com/hc/en-us/articles/360025438734-Apple-Health-Integration" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Oura, Apple Health integration</a>;
  <a href="https://support.ouraring.com/hc/en-us/articles/10786105824531-Health-Connect-by-Android-Integration" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Oura, Health Connect by Android</a>.
</p>

<h3 id="garmin" class="scroll-mt-8 vrv-anchor mt-10 pt-2 text-lg font-semibold text-text-primary">Garmin</h3>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  To Apple Health, by Garmin&rsquo;s support article read on 2 November 2026: workouts, heart rate
  through the day, sleep, steps, active energy, weight and body fat. To Health Connect, by the list
  Garmin gave when it added the integration: body fat, calories burned, floors climbed, heart rate,
  sleep, steps and step distance, weight, and workout data such as elevation and speed; not running
  power, cadence or sweat loss. Garmin keeps HRV status, Body Battery, stress, VO&#8322;max, pulse ox
  and breathing rate in Garmin Connect, so none of those rows fill from a Garmin on either phone;
  a resting heart rate type is not on either list, and a sleep stage split reached Apple Health in
  Verve&rsquo;s checks but is not confirmed for Health Connect. Sources: Garmin Connect, Apple Health
  support article;
  <a href="https://www.tomsguide.com/wellness/fitness-trackers/garmin-clarifies-what-data-its-sharing-with-google-health-connect-heres-the-full-list" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Garmin&rsquo;s Health Connect list, as reported</a>.
</p>

<h3 id="fitbit" class="scroll-mt-8 vrv-anchor mt-10 pt-2 text-lg font-semibold text-text-primary">Fitbit and Pixel Watch, through the Google Health app</h3>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  The Fitbit app is now the Google Health app, and its Health Connect table is published. It
  writes: steps, speed and step cadence, floors, distance, elevation, exercise with its route,
  total calories burned, VO&#8322;max, sleep sessions and stages, skin temperature, heart rate, heart
  rate variability, breathing rate, resting heart rate, weight and body fat. It reads blood oxygen
  and active calories from Health Connect but writes neither, so from a Fitbit or a Pixel Watch
  Verve has no blood oxygen row and no active energy: a session&rsquo;s MET takes the Compendium
  value and physical activity level is not read (<a href={activityLevel}>why PAL needs active and
  resting energy</a>). On an iPhone the Google Health app writes steps, sleep, vitals and exercise
  to Apple Health from its August 2026 release; which vitals exactly is not published. Source:
  <a href="https://support.google.com/fitbit/answer/14506680" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Health, Health Connect data types</a>.
</p>

<h3 id="galaxy-watch" class="scroll-mt-8 vrv-anchor mt-10 pt-2 text-lg font-semibold text-text-primary">Galaxy Watch, through Samsung Health</h3>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  Android only: a Galaxy Watch does not pair with an iPhone. Samsung Health writes to Health
  Connect: steps by the minute, heart rate including exercise heart rate, exercise sessions, sleep
  with stages, blood oxygen, weight, body fat, height and basal metabolic rate. Resting heart rate,
  HRV, breathing rate, skin temperature and VO&#8322;max are not on its mapping. Because its steps
  arrive by the minute, a Galaxy phone with Samsung Health is the Android setup on which cadence,
  sitting hours and sitting bouts read as they do on an iPhone (<a href={steps}>steps</a>). Sources:
  <a href="https://developer.samsung.com/health/blog/en/accessing-samsung-health-data-through-health-connect" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Samsung, accessing Samsung Health data through Health Connect</a>;
  <a href="https://developer.samsung.com/health/health-connect-faq.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Samsung, Health Connect FAQ</a>.
</p>

<h3 id="gaps" class="scroll-mt-8 vrv-anchor mt-10 pt-2 text-lg font-semibold text-text-primary">What Verve does with a gap</h3>
<div id="only-what-arrives" class="mt-3 scroll-mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-ink/8 bg-ink/4 p-6">
  <p class="font-mono text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-accent-light">Only what your device shares</p>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">A row is on the screen only while something sends it.</strong>
    Until 14 November 2026 a reading no device supplied sat on its card as a dash with a reason, so
    a person wearing a band that writes six readings looked at nine dashes every day. Now the app
    decides, reading by reading, by what has actually arrived in the health store, never by the
    name of the device:
  </p>
  <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <li><strong class="text-text-primary">Arrives.</strong> The reading has come in during the last 30 days, from any device or app. Its row is shown.</li>
    <li><strong class="text-text-primary">Usually arrives, missing now.</strong> A night not worn, a flat battery, a sync not yet done. The row stays, with a dash and the reason: a gap is shown as a gap.</li>
    <li><strong class="text-text-primary">Does not arrive.</strong> Nothing has sent it in 30 days. The row leaves the app&rsquo;s Activity and Trends pages, and is listed once, with the reason, under Settings &rarr; Devices.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    The day a reading first arrives, from a new watch or a maker that starts sharing more, its row
    returns by itself. Nothing is left off before the app has finished its first look at the store,
    because an empty store and a slow one look the same. A switch in Settings &rarr; Devices, Show
    them anyway, puts every row back as a dash with its reason.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    What you supply is never left off: the fitness test, blood pressure, weight, blood results and
    logged sessions wait for you, not for a device. Verve does not put an estimate in a missing
    reading&rsquo;s place, and does not suggest another device.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 text-xs leading-relaxed text-text-tertiary">
    The table above is what to expect from each maker; the app does not use it. Thirty days is a
    display rule of Verve&rsquo;s own. Where Back up watch readings is on in the app, the list
    travels with the week&rsquo;s figures, so My health and the Monday email leave off the same
    rows; where it is off, they hold what you typed in and the reports you uploaded.
  </p>
</div>
<p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  A reading that arrives and is missing today shows as a dash, never a zero, and the row says why
  when it can. Where two wearables write the same
  reading, one leads and the other counts where it is alone; Settings &rarr; Data sources names
  which, per reading, and lets you choose. Steps and energy are one writer&rsquo;s count a day,
  never two added together. Workout energy is read for what the writer&rsquo;s figure holds, rest
  included or not, before the session is classed. And physical activity level is withheld rather
  than guessed when every energy writer in the window is workout-shaped.
</p>
